LIBPKGCORE(7)

# NAME

libpkgcore - package database and archive manipulation library

# DESCRIPTION

libpkgcore is a C++ library providing primitives for:

- package database management
- package installation and removal
- filesystem-level operations
- archive inspection and extraction

The library is stateful and exception-driven.

# ARCHITECTURE

The system is composed of three layers:

- db_lock: directory-level locking using RAII
- fs_utils: low-level filesystem helpers
- pkgutil: package database and transaction logic

pkgutil is the central component and maintains an in-memory
representation of the package database.

# DATA MODEL

The package database is represented as:

- packages_t: map<string, pkginfo_t>
- pkginfo_t:
	- version (string)
	- files (set<string>)

All operations mutate or query this structure.

# EXECUTION MODEL

Typical flow:

. acquire lock (db_lock)
. pkgutil::db_open()
. perform operations
. pkgutil::db_commit()

Failure to commit results in lost state.

# ERROR HANDLING

All high-level operations throw std::runtime_error on failure.

Lower-level utilities may return boolean status.

No errno contract is provided.

# SIGNALS AND INTERRUPTION

*libpkgcore* does not install signal handlers or alter signal masks.
Constructing a library object has no process-wide signal side effects.

Signal handling is process policy and remains the responsibility of
the consumer.
A command-line frontend may defer termination while a filesystem and
database mutation sequence is in progress.
A large orchestrator may instead translate signals into its own
cancellation policy.

The current mutating primitives do not provide transactional rollback.
Deferring signals prevents one class of interruption, but does not
protect against process crashes, *SIGKILL*, I/O failures, or power
loss.
Consumers must not present signal deferral as an atomicity guarantee.

# THREAD SAFETY

The library is not thread-safe.

External synchronization is required.

# SEE ALSO

*db\_lock*(3),
*fs\_utils*(3),
*pkgutil*(3)

